{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793745463,"nonce":"fgDYQuGcd9Ay4HjqN0We4Sl/afI=","message":"Frame buffer reuse in 693a5fd2eef1a8a4f57b2cc3d0d95b6fd13a242c5ba029a5d6b118433e866778 removes almost all non-GC allocations from warmed recursive calls, but ordinary release throughput remains near baseline (158.57 -\u003e 157.62 ns/frame without roots; 204.58 -\u003e 208.46 ns with roots). The allocation-audit timings exaggerate the benefit of removing allocations and must not be used as the speed verdict.\n\nA separate post-reuse NUC perf run over recursive calls, roots off then on, samples run_frame at 36.77%, call_by_id at 15.35%, value drop glue at 7.16%, Vec\u003cZyntaxValue\u003e initialization at 4.89%, two value-clone symbols totaling 6.95%, and memmove at 5.13%. These shares are diagnostic and overlap the conceptual work done by inlined code; they are not predicted speedups. Value width, setup/copy/drop work, and call bookkeeping remain worth targeting. Smaller reusable frame metadata may also avoid copying the collection of Vec headers, but has not been measured.\n\nAll 17 normal LLVM-enabled Lua benchmarks and three short Python kernels (float, fannkuch, nbody_modified; five iterations) retain correct reference outputs. Aggregate wall-time ratios are essentially unchanged. Evidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-frame-storage/nuc/{micro.json,profile.txt,lua.json,python.json}. Timing used ordinary release binaries, 15 alternating microbenchmark rounds and seven alternating frontend rounds, affinity 0,2,4,6, with no Cargo/rustc running during or between samples.","files":null}]}